WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.500
Socket code has a lifecycle.

2
00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:14.280
A program creates a descriptor or handle, configures it, binds or connects it, reads and writes through it, shuts it down when communication is finished, closes it, and cleans up any associated state.

3
00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:21.900
Security and reliability improve when that lifecycle is explicit rather than scattered across unrelated helper functions.

4
00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:25.620
Every successful socket creation should have a clear owner.

5
00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:33.560
That owner is responsible for closing the descriptor, releasing buffers, unregistering event-loop state, and handling errors.

6
00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:36.060
In a simple function this may be obvious.

7
00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:46.480
In a threaded service, event-driven server, or helper-based codebase, ownership can become unclear quickly unless it is documented and reflected in the code structure.

8
00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:50.840
Error paths need the same discipline as the successful path.

9
00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:55.660
If configuration fails after socket creation, the descriptor should be closed.

10
00:00:55.760 --> 00:01:02.080
If accepting a connection succeeds but allocating connection state fails, the accepted descriptor should not leak.

11
00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:08.180
If a worker thread takes ownership, the handoff should be visible and single-owner behavior should remain clear.

12
00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:11.340
Shutdown and close behavior should be deliberate.

13
00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:20.560
A program may need to signal that it will not send more data, continue reading remaining data, or close immediately because the peer violated the protocol.

14
00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:26.420
The right behavior depends on the protocol and platform, but it should be a conscious choice rather than an accidental side effect.

15
00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:29.340
Socket options also deserve review.

16
00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:39.360
Options related to reuse, buffering, blocking mode, keepalive, timeouts, address families, and inheritance can change security and reliability behavior.

17
00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:45.380
Reviewers should be able to understand why each option is set and how it supports the intended lifecycle.
